= Salmon

Status: not classical in the three Eurasian traditions for salmon specifically. Atlantic salmon (Salmo salar) and the Pacific Oncorhynchus species are principally cold-water North Atlantic and North Pacific fish, marginal to the South Asian classical canon (which treats fish under the general matsya category without species-specific salmon attribution) and to classical Chinese pharmacy (which has its own freshwater and coastal-fish drugs but no classical Pacific-salmon entry). The substantive traditions are: (a) indigenous Pacific Northwest North American (Salishan, Chinookan, Haida, Tlingit) — for whom salmon is a foundational dietary, ceremonial, and spiritual substance; (b) Japanese (鮭 sake / shake) — fully integrated into Japanese cuisine and folk dietetics; (c) Northern European (British, Irish, Scandinavian, Russian) — long tradition of fresh, smoked, salted, and fermented preparations. Modern Western pharmacology has rehabilitated salmon as the canonical omega-3-rich oily fish.

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*The species:*
- *_Salmo salar_* L., family Salmonidae — Atlantic salmon; anadromous; spawns in North Atlantic rivers from Maine to Labrador, Greenland, Iceland, Norway, Russia, Britain, Ireland, France.
- *_Oncorhynchus_ species* — Pacific salmon:
  - _O. tshawytscha_ — Chinook / King salmon (largest)
  - _O. nerka_ — Sockeye / Red salmon
  - _O. kisutch_ — Coho / Silver salmon
  - _O. gorbuscha_ — Pink / Humpback salmon
  - _O. keta_ — Chum / Dog salmon
  - _O. masou_ — Masu / Cherry salmon (East Asian — Japanese, Russian Far East, Korean)

*Atlantic vs. Pacific*: a substantial natural-history distinction — the genera diverged ~20 Mya, with different biology, life cycle, and meat. Atlantic salmon is the principal aquaculture species; Pacific salmon is dominantly wild-caught.

*Latin / English:* _Salmo salar_ — _Salmo_ from Latin _salmō_ (the Latin name for salmon, source of English "salmon"); _salar_ is the Roman name for the leaping fish, possibly from _salire_ ("to leap"). English _salmon_ from Old French _saumon_ from Latin _salmonem_.

*Pacific Northwest indigenous languages:* multiple languages have rich salmon vocabularies — Halkomelem Salish _sthq̓éy̓_, Chinook Jargon _sámun_ (a Chinook reborrowing from English), Lushootseed terms for distinct species. The classical *Coast Salish "First Salmon Ceremony"* recognizes the first-caught salmon of the season as a returning relative.

*Japanese:* 鮭 (sake / shake) — the canonical Japanese word, principally referring to _Oncorhynchus keta_ (chum) in classical use; the broader Pacific salmon-family. 紅鮭 (benizake) — sockeye. 鮭の塩漬け (shio-zuke) — salt-cured; 鮭の燻製 (kunsei) — smoked.

*Sanskrit:* No species-specific Sanskrit name. The general fish category is मत्स्य (_matsya_); the saṃhitās distinguish freshwater (_nādī-matsya_) and marine (_samudra-matsya_) fish without identifying salmon-equivalent species. Salmon as a substance has no classical Sanskrit identification.

*Chinese:* 鮭魚 (guīyú / xiéyú, "salmon") — historically the character 鮭 referred variously to puffer fish or salmon; modern usage 鮭魚 unambiguous for salmon. 三文魚 (sānwényú) is the Cantonese-derived modern phonetic transliteration.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_No classical Chinese entry for Pacific or Atlantic salmon as such; reasoned cross-system placement only._

By reasoned placement (consistent with the Chinese-medical reading of oily fish): warm (溫); sweet (甘); Spleen, Stomach, Kidney channels. Reasoned actions: 補虛 (tonifies vacuity), 益氣 (augments qi), 補腎 (tonifies Kidney), 暖中 (warms the middle). Useful in cold-pattern vacuity, postpartum, debility. Cautions in damp-heat patterns. The Japanese folk-dietetic register treats salmon as warming and tonifying, consistent with this reasoning.#footnote[Reasoned cross-system placement; the Japanese folk-dietetic register is the closest substantive tradition.]

== Indigenous Pacific Northwest tradition

This is the most substantive ethnographically-documented salmon tradition.

For the Salishan, Chinookan, Haida, Tlingit, Nuu-chah-nulth, and other Pacific Northwest peoples, salmon is *not merely a food* but a foundational element of cosmology, economy, ceremony, and ethics.

- *First Salmon Ceremony*: the first-caught salmon of the season is honored with prescribed handling (typically cooked on cedar planks, eaten by the whole community, bones returned to the river in correct orientation); the salmon is conceptualized as a *visiting relative* / spirit-person who chooses to give itself for the people's nourishment.
- *Smoked salmon*: traditional preservation by smoking over alder wood; cedar-plank cooking; pit-roasting.
- *Salmon roe / eggs (ikura in Japanese, salmon caviar)*: prized, with ceremonial uses.
- *Salmon as economic foundation*: the salmon harvest historically supported large Northwest Coast populations and the complex social structures (potlatch, chieftainship) that depended on surplus food.
- *Modern restoration ecology*: the depleted Pacific salmon runs are a major ecological and indigenous-rights issue; treaty fishing rights and salmon restoration are foundational to Northwest political ecology.#footnote[Pacific Northwest indigenous salmon traditions: extensive ethnographic literature — Stewart, _Indian Fishing: Early Methods on the Northwest Coast_ (1977); Hilary Stewart, _Cedar_; Suttles, _Coast Salish Essays_; and contemporary indigenous-authored salmon literature.]

== Japanese tradition

- *Sake/shake (鮭)* as one of the canonical Japanese fish.
- *Shiozake (塩鮭)* — salt-cured salmon; breakfast staple with rice and miso soup.
- *Salmon teriyaki, sashimi, sushi* — modern Japanese preparations; note that *raw salmon was traditionally avoided in Japan due to parasite risk* and only entered Japanese sushi in the 1980s through Norwegian export marketing.
- *Salmon roe (ikura イクラ)*: from Russian _ikra_ (caviar) via Russian-Japanese contact; salt-cured prized delicacy.

== Northern European tradition

- *Smoked salmon* — Scottish, Irish, Norwegian, Danish, Russian traditions; cold-smoked (lox) and hot-smoked variants.
- *Gravlax (Scandinavian)* — salt-and-sugar-and-dill-cured raw salmon; the classical Nordic preparation.
- *Lox (Jewish-American)* — salt-cured salmon; bagel-and-lox tradition is Eastern-European Jewish via New York.
- *Salmon as ritual / status food* — the salmon-fishing aristocratic tradition (Atlantic salmon as the "king of fish" in British river-fishing culture).

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_No specific classical placement; the general_ matsya _placement applies._

By Caraka and Bhāvaprakāśa on _matsya_ (fish) generally: madhura rasa; guru, snigdha guṇa; uṣṇa vīrya (some sources śīta for freshwater); madhura vipāka. Pacifies Vāta; aggravates Kapha and Pitta. _Balya, bṛṃhaṇa, vṛṣya._ Cautions in damp-heat / inflammatory / skin disease; the famous *fish + milk saṃyoga viruddha* — fish and milk together classically forbidden (Caraka). For oily fish like salmon, the more snigdha-uṣṇa register applies; appropriate for Vāta-pacification and convalescent nourishment.#footnote[Fish (matsya) in Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (_annapānavidhi_, fish section); Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 46; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Māṃsa-varga_. The fish-milk viruddha is one of the most-cited saṃyoga viruddhas in the saṃhitās.]

=== Viruddha (Modern)

- *(S/N) Fish + milk* — classical saṃyoga viruddha; observed in Indian dietetics.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Mercury*: salmon is *low-mercury* (much lower than tuna, swordfish, king mackerel, shark); among the safest fish for mercury exposure in pregnancy and pediatric use.
  - *PCBs / dioxins*: farmed salmon has historically had higher PCB levels than wild; modern aquaculture practice has reduced but not eliminated the concern.
  - *Antibiotics*: salmon farming uses antibiotics; reputable certifications (ASC, MSC for wild) provide some assurance.
  - *Parasites*: anisakid nematodes in raw or undercooked fish; freezing or cooking destroys.
  - *Allergy*: fish allergy is among the major food allergies.
  - *Sustainability*: Atlantic salmon wild populations are depleted; Pacific salmon vary by run and species; sustainable certification matters.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

Salmon is *not species-specific in classical Tibb*. The medieval Arabic medical tradition treats fish generically as *samak* (سمك), with species distinguished only at the river-vs-sea / large-vs-small / scale-vs-no-scale axis; the cold-water North Atlantic and Pacific salmon were outside the medieval Arab geographical reach. For the general Tibb *samak* framework — Hot-Moist mizāj of most oily fish; nourishing, aphrodisiac, with *muḥarrik al-akhlāṭ* humor-stirring tendency; mistrust of unfresh fish; canonical pairing with vinegar / lemon / coriander as muṣliḥ; classical fish-milk incompatibility (parallel to the Ayurvedic _fish-milk saṃyoga viruddha_); kashrut-canonical fins-and-scales rule preserved in Shi'i halal — see _fish.typ_. Salmon-specific use is modern, by extension. The *samak salmūn* modern Arabic name is direct phonetic transliteration.

Composition, salmon, raw, approximate per 100 g (Atlantic farmed; wild and species variation substantial) — verify against USDA FoodData Central.

- Energy ~210 kcal; Water ~64 g
- Protein ~20 g (high quality, complete amino-acid profile)
- *Fat ~13 g (varies widely: 5–20 g by species and farming)*
- *Long-chain omega-3 (EPA + DHA) ~2.0–2.5 g per 100 g* — among the highest in commonly-consumed fish; the principal modern nutritional claim
- Vitamin D ~10–25 µg per 100 g — *one of the few naturally rich dietary sources of vitamin D* in cold-water oily fish
- Vitamin B12 ~3 µg
- Selenium ~36 µg
- *Astaxanthin* — the pink-orange carotenoid (from the salmon's diet of krill and crustaceans, or — in farmed — fed as synthetic or yeast-derived astaxanthin)
- Choline; B6; niacin

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Long-chain omega-3 (EPA, DHA)*: cardiovascular-supportive (modest cardiovascular benefit from regular oily-fish consumption in meta-analyses); triglyceride-lowering at higher doses; modest anti-inflammatory; potentially helpful in mood disorders (preliminary).
- *Vitamin D*: meaningful contribution to vitamin D status, especially in temperate climates where solar synthesis is reduced.
- *Astaxanthin*: powerful antioxidant; preliminary preclinical and clinical evidence for skin and cardiovascular endpoints; modest.
- *Protein quality*: complete; suitable for muscle synthesis.
- *Cross-system convergence*: the Japanese, Northern European, Pacific Northwest indigenous, and modern omega-3 traditions converge on salmon as a *nourishing, strengthening, vitalizing* food — entirely consistent with the modern composition.

*Safe daily / weekly intake:*
- *General adults*: 1–2 servings (~100–200 g each) per week of oily fish is the standard recommendation (American Heart Association, NHS).
- *Pregnant women*: 2–3 servings/week of low-mercury fish including salmon is recommended (US FDA / EPA, NHS).
- *Children*: appropriate at smaller portions.
- *Wild vs. farmed*: both are nutritionally valuable; wild has lower PCB; farmed has higher omega-3 by mass but variable. Sourcing matters.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*

